# Exocortex — Master Plan

**Status (2026-10-08, v0.5.1 SHIPPED — the store-crash-tolerance
patch):** D50 root-caused live on the owner's machine and fixed (the
remaining module forks SIGILL the supervised darwin store's parent; see
the D50 row). Version bumped (+ openapi golden regenerated), the full
`verify-release.sh` matrix green on the exact release tree (one
D36-class contention flake on `failing_spawn_kills_the_store_group`
under back-to-back full-matrix load — green standalone and in the
passing rerun; the publish run's internal matrix hit the same flake
once, also green on rerun), tag v0.5.1 pushed (release.yml: correctness
+ all three platform builds + assets green), and ALL 17 crates
published to crates.io at 0.5.1 (publish exit 0). Release-note honesty,
as PUBLISHING records: the runtime can still SIGILL one-to-two times
per boot window — the supervisor's bounded restarts and the ingest
retry ride through it, but the permanent fix is an upstream falkordblite
darwin-arm64 rebuild (npm ships only 8.2.3-falkordb.4.16.2 and .3);
live Falkor/Redis/Postgres/CDC legs and the SaaS adapter live suites
not executed; compose chaos not run; D50's real-graph reproduction and
verification ran on the owner's machine (this cut ships its fixes).

**Post-0.5.1 (2026-10-10, since pushed — see the 2026-10-11 entry):** D51 — standalone
project-visibility rows were written, accepted, and then never
readable from a new session (the owner's live bug report from Claude
Code) — fixed in f52152e with unit + e2e tests and a live
repro verification on the real personal graph; the standalone restart
budget rides 3 → 512 in the same commit (the report's "node stopped
silently" symptom was budget exhaustion under clustered SIGILLs, not a
new crash class). D52 — SSE response headers withheld behind the seed
scan (issue #8's intermittent "SSE response headers timed out"
degradation) — fixed in 8c68a3d: the scan moved inside the response
stream behind the anchor comment, the seed-build phase is now logged,
and the ordering is pinned by a latch-driven test through the real Sse
body. A 0.5.2 cut is pending the owner's call.

**2026-10-11/12 (post-crash triage):** the mid-release "system crash"
was session-level (the machine never went down); v0.5.1 was already
fully shipped — tag, release assets, all 17 crates at 0.5.1 — before
the session died. The stranded D51/D52 commits passed a fresh full
matrix on the exact tree (exit 0) and were pushed (51aa32b..6b4dda2)
under the standing authorization. That same matrix run is the first
full post-REL2 LOCAL measurement — 45,552s (12.65h) — recorded as
**REL4 (Backlog, Proposed)**: lever 1 (single-execution trust, −~4.6h
of duplicated suite execution) parks on the owner's gate-semantics
call; levers 2-3 (nextest locally, contention hygiene — quit Docker
Desktop at cut time) are the locally-satisfiable continuation of the
owner's gate-speed ask; lever 4 (cold-compile caching) serves CI and
crash-recovery reruns only. A 0.5.2 cut remains pending the owner's
call. The REL4 row and this entry are local-only plan commits riding
the next gated push. Also observed live: the standalone node
restarted ~21:54 mid-matrix, and a long-lived MCP client holding the
pre-restart port stayed connection-refused (frozen reads, failed
writes) until its session restarted — consistent with the D46/D52
boundaries, but if it reproduces with a CURRENT client it deserves
its own row (the client may not re-resolve the node port after a
wrapper restart).

**2026-10-12/13 (REL4 landed; clean re-measurement parks on a quiet
box):** REL4's levers 1-3 landed and closed (d33b861) — the owner took
both parked calls (single-execution trust; nextest-by-default), the
full matrix ran green on the exact tree (exit 0), and both commits are
pushed. The closing measurement is HONEST BUT CONTENDED: 40,183s total
under sustained load from parallel loanlight agent sessions (every one
of the 822 nextest-driven tests paid ~112-144s of darwin spawn tax),
so it bounds nothing about the levers' ceiling — the structural wins
are the phase table itself (seam-inventory 9,008→1s, adapter-contract
3,987→1s, write-path-parity 1,312→376s churn-only, rel1-sweep
1,996→597s; CI runs the identical content in 16m39s across four
shards). Owner direction at close: the box is under heavy load right
now (load avg ~19 of 24 cores, loanlight sessions churning), so the
uncontended re-measurement PARKS until the machine is quiet — and the
next full matrix run should serve double duty as the 0.5.2 pre-release
gate (one run, two purposes; the 0.5.2 cut itself remains pending the
owner's call). If the quiet-box run is still slow, the visible
next suspects are inter-phase compile churn from feature flips
(acceptance-coverage and deployment-acceptance each carried 30-45min
recompiles) — not more trust. Companion fix pushed in the same range:
D53 (65fc6f2) — `--verify`'s store row stopped false-REDing on
sibling data-dir path prefixes (found triaging the owner's dead
memory connection; the operational fault itself — orphaned standalone
stacks holding the WAL lock — was cleared on the owner's machine
during triage).

**2026-10-14 (power-outage recovery; D56 root-caused — not the data):**
the outage lost nothing (D55 had closed clean at 522465b; eight local
commits — six pre-outage plus today's D56 (38c9611) / D57 (b5339a6)
pair — now ride the next gated matrix, which parks while the box is
this loaded). D56's "poisoned graph rows" hypothesis
is REFUTED: reproduced deterministically on a copy of the loanlight
data — the hydration count query `count_state_at_rels` was O(rows^2)
in FalkorDB (aggregate, then rescan-by-id per group), taking 523ms at
2,477 assertion rows, and the falkordb 0.3.0 crate rides redis 1.2.2
whose DEFAULT async response timeout is 500ms — every slower graph
command dies as an opaque ConnectionDown (personal-org hydrates only
because its 586 rows take 31ms; same curve, under the line). Recorded
as deviation 23 with row D58 open for the durable client-side fix.
D56's fix is a single-pass max-by-list-key rewrite (3ms, 162x), pinned
by a live-backend integration test that ConnectionDowns on the pre-fix
tree. Companion defect D57 fell out of the same e2e: an idle BLMOVE
answers nil, which the Dreams fire drain decoded straight to String —
one TypeError warning per second on every quiet store (visible live on
loanlight, masked on personal by traffic); its fix landed in the paired
commit (b5339a6). End-to-end on a copy of the real
7.3M loanlight data with this tree's binaries: zero hydration
failures, zero warnings. The `~/loanlight/.mcp.json` personal-org
workaround STAYS until a release installs the fix (0.5.2 still pending
the owner's call).

**2026-10-17 (0.5.2 SHIPPED; Blacksmith + CI-attested publish landed):**
all 17 crates published to crates.io at 0.5.2 (publish exit 0, "All
Exocortex crates published"), closing the multi-day release: the
gates were carried by CI on the exact tree (run 38758603256 on
cf8239e; the local matrix was unwinnable — the box's assessment queue
wedged three matrix runs and one publish, and even the attested
publish's first `cargo metadata` wedged 45 minutes before a calm
window let it run through; the tag/release workflow had been green
since 2026-10-15). Two durable fixes landed and were dogfooded
same-day, both CI-green on the FIRST Blacksmith run (PR #9 merged —
runner tags validated against Blacksmith's docs; Intel macOS stays on
GitHub runners, ubuntu-2204 preserved for the linux build's
userland): **REL5** `PUBLISH_VERIFY=ci` attests CI's green gate shards
on the exact HEAD and lets publish.sh proceed without the local
matrix (fail-closed; `scripts/ci-attestation.sh`), and **REL6** the
spawn-health canary heads verify-release.sh so a degraded queue fails
the matrix in seconds with the remediation instead of wedging it for
hours. Honest notes for the record: the attested publish itself had
to survive the queue (the canary would have refused the LOCAL path —
correctly); REL4's quiet-box re-measurement is still unexecuted (the
queue makes local timing runs unreliable; CI's Blacksmith timings are
now the stable reference); the local matrix remains the default
publish path, with the attestation as the degraded-box escape hatch.
Next candidate (owner call): a release.yml publish job on Blacksmith
runners with the registry token in Actions secrets — publishing fully
off the laptop. The 0.5.2 installer/runtime carries D56/D57 —
repointing `~/loanlight/.mcp.json` at the loanlight-org graph is now
safe after `exocortex --version` shows 0.5.2.

**2026-10-18 (0.5.3 SHIPPED — the fork-safe darwin runtime):** all 17
crates at 0.5.3 on crates.io, tag v0.5.3 at 0b49d06, release run
39157774882 fully green — correctness, all three platform builds (the
darwin-arm64 build packaged the D59 fork module and validated it
natively on the runner), asset release, and the REL7 CI-side publish,
which exercised the whole new machinery end to end (attestation →
publish.sh → 17 crates) for the first time. Release-day fixes, all
gates doing their jobs: the publish job needed protoc, the fixtures
gate refused a mutable apt install so protoc 36.2 is digest-pinned
from its immutable release (with the well-known protos extracted —
the first cut dropped them and wire's tarball verification could not
compile timestamp.proto), and the asset-integrity guard correctly
refused non-identical overwrites when the tag moved to the fixed
tree (the stale release object was deleted and rebuilt; the
correctness shard also flaked once on a worker-fixture transport
connect — green on rerun, the D36 class). The tag moved twice
pre-announcement (69a2333 → ac17c14 → 0b49d06); the 4 crates
published by the first attempt were skipped as already-published by
the final run. Local install done (0.5.3 binaries + the pinned fork
module bdc47d95…9ce8); a live session booted on it same-hour. The
first fully-laptop-independent release: nothing in the chain touched
the release machine.

## Archived status history

Every superseded status entry (2026-08-25 through 2026-10-06), the
wave narratives, and the historical baselines now live in
[master-plan-archive.prd](master-plan-archive.prd) — same repository,
append-only, unchanged. The D-row table below remains the sole
authority record (fingerprint-move authorization lives in rows, not
status paragraphs).

