| REL6 | **A degraded macOS assessment queue wedges the local matrix for hours with no diagnosis at the point of wedge** — the 0.5.2 cut's live finding (2026-10-14/16): a freshly-built binary's FIRST exec can sit in dyld behind a flooded syspolicyd indefinitely (zero CPU, `_dyld_start` in every sample; `codesign -v` itself blocked 2m05s), and verify-release.sh walked straight into it three times — the test phase stalls silently until someone notices hours later. Fix shape: a first-spawn health canary at the TOP of the matrix — compile a tiny fresh binary, time its first exec, and fail fast with the remediation (reboot the box or publish via REL5's CI attestation) when it exceeds a bound | 0.5.2 release incident, 2026-10-14/16 | **Done (2026-10-17, this commit)** — the canary heads verify-release.sh (darwin-only; `--crate-name` is explicit because macOS mktemp names carry dots rustc rejects as crate names): it compiles a never-before-seen `fn main(){}`, times the first exec against `EXOCORTEX_SPAWN_CANARY_MAX_SECS` (default 30s), prints the assessment time on the phase table when healthy, and exits 1 with the remediation (reboot, or `PUBLISH_VERIFY=ci`) when exceeded — before fmt or any compile work starts. `EXOCORTEX_SKIP_SPAWN_CANARY=1` opts out; CI's linux runners never take the branch. Verified both ways: `EXOCORTEX_SPAWN_CANARY_MAX_SECS=0` refuses with the remediation and kills the canary (exit 1); the default bound passes at 1s on a healthy queue |
| REL5 | **Publishing is chained to a healthy release machine — `publish.sh` fail-closed runs the full LOCAL verify-release matrix before any upload** — the 0.5.2 cut's structural finding: CI had already run the identical gate set green on the exact tree, the tag and release assets were green, and the crates.io upload still could not proceed for days because the local matrix re-runs everything and the box's assessment queue (see REL6) wedges it. Fix shape: a `PUBLISH_VERIFY=ci` attestation mode — verify via `gh` that a completed-success `ci` workflow run exists for the EXACT HEAD sha with every gate shard green, then upload without the local run; fail-closed preserved (no green run on the exact tree → refuse). The local matrix stays the default | 0.5.2 release incident, 2026-10-16 | **Done (2026-10-17, this commit)** — `scripts/ci-attestation.sh` (expands the sha, requires a completed-success `ci` run whose headSha equals the tree, then verifies every gate shard — lint/tests/fixtures/xtask-gates — is green on that run; any gap refuses) and the `PUBLISH_VERIFY=ci` branch in `publish.sh` ahead of the local-matrix default; documented in PUBLISHING.md. Verified both ways against the live repo: attests cf8239e (the 0.5.2 tree, green shards), refuses a fabricated sha and any tree CI has not passed (`refused: no completed-success ci run on this commit`); the refusal paths print their reason and exit 1 before any manifest mutation |
| REL4 | **Local gate-matrix wall-time, round 2 — the first full post-REL2 local measurement** — a warm-target-dir matrix on the owner's 24-core darwin box took **45,552s (12.65h)**: test-workspace-run 23,569s (52%); seam-inventory 9,008s; adapter-contract 3,987s; rel1-compile-sweep 1,996s; deployment-acceptance 1,791s; storage-conformance 1,701s; write-path-parity 1,312s; artifact-equivalence 588s; auth-coverage 395s; bench 5s; every compile phase ≤12s warm (fmt 1s, clippy 2s, fastembed 2s). Structural findings: (1) **~16.6ks (36%) is suite re-EXECUTION duplicated from test-workspace-run** — seam-inventory re-runs all ~60 seam suites as serialized single-suite cargo spawns (main.rs `seam_inventory` loop), storage-conformance re-runs the full `-p exocortex-storage` suite, adapter-contract re-runs 4 suites, write-path-parity 1 — all on the same feature set and byte-identical binaries the workspace phase just executed; REL2's deferred "single-execution trust" lever is now MEASURED at ~4.6h, still needing its gate-semantics decision (the gates' real static assertions — bijection, canaries, listings — cost 1-3s each and must stay; only the redundant execution is on the table). (2) **CI runs everything in 16m39s where local takes 45,552s (~27x)**: (a) the local default is cargo test (serial test-binary execution; nextest not installed locally — REL2 shipped the opt-in path but left the local default); (b) sustained contention during the measured run (Docker Desktop VM at ~37% of a core for the entire run, node churn, syspolicyd ~35% — the darwin exec-verification tax hits every one of ~100 test binaries × re-runs); (c) the darwin runtime-booting suites (standalone wrapper/attach boots pay module load + D46b's 25-75s fork-quiesce per boot) that CI NEVER executes (bundled runtime absent on ubuntu runners). Candidate levers, ranked: (1) single-execution trust (−~4.6h, owner semantics call); (2) nextest locally (parallelize the remaining execution across ~100 binaries — CI precedent, plumbing exists); (3) contention hygiene at cut time (quit Docker Desktop; the constant syspolicyd churn is a per-exec tax worth knowing about); (4) cold-compile levers (sccache locally; the REL2-recorded SHA-pinned GHA cross-run cache) — these serve CI and crash-recovery reruns, not the warm local loop. Coverage must not shrink: every gate keeps its static assertion and its suite execution when run standalone; only in-matrix duplication is removable, and each phase's verdicts stay identical | Owner ask 2026-10-11 ("get the release gate to run faster") + the measured matrix run (exit 0, this tree) | **Done (2026-10-12, this commit) — levers 1-3 landed; the owner took both parked calls.** (1) Single-execution trust: verify-release.sh exports `EXOCORTEX_MATRIX_TRUST=1` only after the workspace test phase goes green (set -e guarantees no export on a red phase); the four duplicate-executing gates then run ONLY their static assertions in-matrix, each printing an explicit "suite execution trusted from the matrix workspace phase" line (`gates::matrix_trusts_workspace_runs`, exact-`"1"` fail-closed matcher, `matrix_trust_marker_is_exact_and_fail_closed`); standalone invocations never see the marker and always execute suites (verified live: untrusted `write-path-parity` ran its suite, trusted did not). Live legs (FALKOR_URL/REDIS_URL) are not workspace-covered and still run/skip loudly in-matrix. (2) nextest-by-default: the matrix auto-uses cargo-nextest when installed (`EXOCORTEX_TEST_RUNNER=cargo` opts out; absent-binary fallback unchanged); cargo-nextest 0.9.148 installed locally, SHA-256-verified from the official release (cargo install is policy-blocked in agent shells). This exercised REL2's opt-in branch for the first time and caught a dead-code defect: `--profile ci` — no such profile exists in 0.9.148 and CI's proven invocation never passes one; dropped to match CI. (3) Contention hygiene: preflight warning when Docker Desktop is running (advisory, never fatal; it fired in the measured run). **Measured after (as-run, exit 0, this tree, log /tmp/matrix-rel4.log): TOTAL 40,183s** — seam-inventory 9,008→**1s**, adapter-contract 3,987→**1s**, write-path-parity 1,312→376s (compile churn only — the trusted line proves zero execution), storage-conformance 1,701→1,247s (in-memory execution trusted; remainder is live-canary listing compiles), rel1-compile-sweep 1,996→597s, test phase now PARALLEL (822 tests / 126 binaries, 48 concurrently vs serial cargo test; doctests split to their own 3,027s phase). **Honest caveat, recorded:** this run was the most contended yet — parallel loanlight agent sessions (tsc at 285% of a core, vitest workers) starved the box for hours, and every one of the 822 tests paid ~112-144s spawn-to-pass (the darwin exec-verification tax at saturation; even trivial unit tests), so the test phase's 26,392s measures the loaded machine, not the levers' ceiling — CI runs the identical content in 16m39s across four shards. An uncontended local re-measurement is the next data point whenever the box is quiet; it is not a precondition of this closure. Owner direction at close (2026-10-12): the re-measurement PARKS while the box is under heavy load (load ~19/24, parallel loanlight sessions), and the next full matrix run doubles as the 0.5.2 pre-release gate — one run, two purposes. **Not taken, recorded:** lever 4 (sccache local / SHA-pinned GHA cross-run cache) stays parked — it serves CI and crash-recovery reruns, not the warm local loop |
| R14 | **Round 14: deep review + fix cycle over the D44–D49 cascade** (`docs/reviews/round-14-review.prd`) — 7 verified Blocking findings (verify false-RED under the wrapper's falkordb layout; --install-block destroying unreadable files; the harness row's two-substring false green; unterminated-marker duplication; the TIME-unbounded SSE error-body read; attach.json's userinfo bypass + shell-injection surface; the attached WAL slot leaking and stranding pending writes) + 26 Advisory (hostile-body injection into logs/agent stamps, banner false-greens, attach.json hygiene, probe hang past its own deadline, 11 test-hardening gaps, false port-file comment, one-impl/cleanup debt, WAL double-decode, unbounded config reads) | deep-pr-review-lean over 7cac12d..4fadfad, 2026-10-02 | **Done (2026-10-02, this commit)** — every Blocking and every Actionable Advisory fixed with named tests (the round doc carries each finding, its fix, and its evidence); two resolved as RECORDED BOUNDARIES with measured justification (the TMPDIR sweep cost 33s/run — reverted; conf-file foreign writers beyond a client-side check). The fix cycle also caught two of its own defects live (the T7 stub quoting false-pass; the cleanup probe printing into the MCP stdout channel). Fast gates green on the closing tree: fmt, clippy -D warnings (client+server), full client suite (+testing features), full server suite (bin incl. the new ungated attach test + testing features) |
| D56 | **The loanlight-org graph data poisons hydration — every boot, every client** — found fixing loanlight wiring (2026-10-13): a fresh mcp-standalone boot on `exocortex-loanlight` (7.3M real data) starts a HEALTHY store (redis log: clean AOF load, FalkorDB module loaded, ready), but the node's first hydration store call (`count_state_at_rels`) dies with a redis connection error on EVERY attempt → SSE 503 → no client ever hydrates (client `InitialHydrationTimeout`; harness sees "Connection closed"). The pre-kill stack (up since 2026-10-11 21:54, spawned by a claude bg-spare) 503'd the same way for new clients — it wasn't age, it's the data. The personal-org graph on the same binary hydrates instantly (discriminating probe 2026-10-13). D45's data-dependent class: something in this graph's rows kills the module query path. WORKAROUND SHIPPED: `~/loanlight/.mcp.json` now points at the personal-org shared graph (the D44-S2 attach mechanism keeps concurrent boots safe), so loanlight sessions have working memory; the 7.3M parks until repair. FIX SHAPE: isolate the poisoning row(s) — binary-search the graph by reading with bounded queries directly against the store (a read-only harness; hydration's query set is the repro), then repair/export-skip/reimport. Gate: none external — locally reproducible | PLT1 loanlight unit, 2026-10-13 | **Done (2026-10-14, this commit) — root cause REFUTED the poisoning hypothesis; it was a slow query crossing a hidden client timeout.** Reproduced deterministically on a scratch copy of the real data dir: `count_state_at_rels` was O(rows^2) in FalkorDB (aggregate max-lsn per id, then a full rescan per group matching id+lsn with no index), taking **523ms at 2,477 relationship assertion rows** (personal-org's 586 rows take 31ms — the same curve, under the line); the falkordb 0.3.0 crate's async pool rides **redis 1.2.2, whose default async response timeout is 500ms** (see deviation 23), so every attempt died as `FalkorDBError::ConnectionDown` — the exact "redis connection error on EVERY attempt". The store was never at fault (byte-identical queries succeed over raw RESP; MONITOR showed the server receiving the query and completing it ~520ms in). Fix: both count templates (`count_state_at`, `count_state_at_rels`) rewritten single-pass — `max([lsn, coalesce(valid_until, sentinel)])` per id, order-independent (a first ORDER-BY-into-collect attempt DIVERGED: FalkorDB's collect() does not preserve ORDER BY order across grouping — caught on a hand-seeded supersession corpus, original=2 vs rewrite=1). 3.2ms on the loanlight copy (162x); equivalence verified against the original query on the synthetic corpus AND the real graph (2477). Fail-without-it: storage integration `count_state_stays_sublinear_under_thousands_of_assertions` (live FALKOR_URL; seeds 4.2k assertion rows with superseded versions; the pre-fix tree ConnectionDowns at `get_state_at`, stash-verified red; post-fix green with a 400ms bound). End-to-end on a copy of the real 7.3M data with this tree's node+client: one clean seed, zero hydration failures, zero warnings (the residual fire-drain spam was D57, fixed separately). NOTE: the shipped `~/loanlight/.mcp.json` personal-org workaround stays until a release installs this fix; D58 tracks the durable client-side timeout problem |
| D57 | **An idle Dreams fire drain warns once per second forever — BLMOVE's nil timeout decoded as String** — found in D56's e2e (2026-10-14): with the hydration blocker gone, the loanlight-org node still logged `fire drain error; retrying ... TypeError: "Response type not string compatible." (response was nil)` every ~6s on a quiet store; `RedisFireQueue::drain` queried BLMOVE into `String` and guarded the idle case with `contains("timeout")`, but a timed-out BLMOVE answers a NIL REPLY, not an error — personal-org never showed it only because its queue never idles the full drain window | D56 e2e, 2026-10-14 | **Done (2026-10-14, paired commit) — the drain decodes `Option<String>`; `None` is the steady-state `TimedOut` (the error-string guard stays for transport timeouts).** Fail-without-it: dreams `fire_live::drain_on_an_idle_queue_times_out_cleanly` (live Redis; panics on the pre-fix tree's TypeError through the `expect`, stash-verified red, passes clean after) |
| D58 | **The falkordb crate's redis 1.x client defaults every graph command to a 500ms response timeout, unconfigurably** — D56's root cause (deviation 23): falkordb 0.3.0 builds its async pool on redis 1.2.2 (`AsyncConnectionConfig::default()` = 500ms) and exposes no override (`with_timeout` is the SERVER-side query timeout); any graph command slower than 500ms fails as an opaque ConnectionDown that reads as store corruption, and the failure mode scales with data. D56 removed today's slow queries; the ceiling itself stays. Durable fix: upstream (a falkordb release passing `AsyncConnectionConfig` through) or a vendored `[patch.crates-io]` fork. Owner decision (rule-9 class): a vendored fork fixes workspace builds but NOT crates.io consumers of the published storage crate — that divergence must be weighed; until then the operating rule is templates stay comfortably under 500ms (D56's test holds the line executable) | D56 root cause, 2026-10-14 | **Proposed** |
| D55 | **A current MCP client does not re-resolve the standalone node after a supervised restart — writes fail `tcp connect error` until the harness session restarts** — the 2026-10-12 header suspected it on an old client; reproduced 2026-10-13 with the CURRENT 0.5.1 client: the personal-org node SIGILL-restarted at 15:17:52 (pid 62641 → 2868, port file 54326 → 56941) mid-session, and this Crush session's exocortex client kept dialing the dead port — `end_session` failed twice with `ingest: Unavailable: tcp connect error` and `backend_status` reports `degraded: true, "Connection refused (os error 61)"` (reads keep serving the pre-restart frozen snapshot, backend_lsn 7233759). Fix shape: on backend connect failure, re-discover the supervised node endpoint (re-read the port file / re-run wrapper discovery) within a bounded retry before surfacing the error | PLT1 session, 2026-10-13 (this session's memory write blocked by it — the draft rides the session transcript) | **Done (2026-10-13, this commit) — re-resolution rides the attach record, both paths.** The attach record moved to `exocortex_wire::attach::AttachInfo` (ONE parse/validate shape for server and client; structural loopback/userinfo/path validation, no new dependency; the R14-B6 matrix moved with it) and gained the data-home line; the owner node publishes `EXOCORTEX_STANDALONE_DATA_HOME` into every session's runtime env (owner and attach paths), the wrapper exports it. The client re-resolves on BOTH paths: end_session acquires its transport through `LiveBackend` (transport-class failure → re-read attach.json → adopt the successor's endpoint AND fresh credentials → RE-SIGN with the new producer key → retry once; plain `--backend` sessions keep the fixed channel) and the SSE reconnect seam adopts between reconnects (fresh endpoint + creds + a forced full reseed — the successor's LSN line is not comparable, so the only safe post-adoption state is the new owner's authoritative image). Fail-without-it: `client/tests/owner_rediscovery.rs::long_lived_client_survives_owner_replacement` — baseline write on owner A, A's whole runtime dropped (a FAITHFUL kill: dropping just the BackendNode closes the listener but leaves detached connection tasks acking submits and heartbeating SSE — the harness lesson recorded below), successor B with different endpoint + entirely fresh credentials, then the SAME long-lived session's end_session lands on B (accepted, LSN>0), a DIRECT probe client proves B's graph serves the row (no zombie), and the session's search finds it after SSE adoption (3.72s, green). LIVE fail-without-it on the pre-fix tree: this session's and a parallel Codex session's writes both failed `ingest: Unavailable: tcp connect error` after a real 15:17 SIGILL restart, with `backend_status` degraded `Connection refused` — the exact repro. **Test-harness lesson (the better-tests ask):** in-process node kills are unfaithful on shared runtimes — each fake owner must own a dedicated `tokio::runtime` so dropping it RSTs every connection; without that, tests green-light zombie-connection behavior the real world never shows. Gates: fmt, clippy -D warnings (wire+server+client), full client suite with testing features (all suites green) |
| D54 | **`--verify` exits 1 with `Error: wal io: … WouldBlock` while a LIVE, healthy standalone stack holds the WAL** — found opening PLT1 (2026-10-13): with the owner's personal-org stack up and serving (three sessions on the wrapper-supervised node), `exocortex-mcp-client --verify` printed its ontology/playbook/hmac/backend rows then DIED on the WAL lock — the checklist cannot distinguish a lock held by a live supervised node (healthy; should render a green/amber row naming the holder pid) from the orphan case it exists to catch (red), so the owner's own diagnostic is unusable exactly when the system is running. Reproduction: run `exocortex-mcp-client --verify` with any healthy standalone stack up | PLT1 wiring audit, 2026-10-13 | **Proposed** |
| D53 | **--verify store row false-REDs on a sibling data dir sharing a path prefix** — found triaging the owner's "exocortex not saving memories" report (2026-10-12; the actual fault was WAL lock + orphaned stacks elsewhere): `store_ports_on_dir` matched the data dir as a plain SUBSTRING of each `ps` command line, so the personal dir `…/Application Support/exocortex` matched the loanlight store's `…/exocortex-loanlight/falkordb` argv — a healthy, live-supervised sibling store read as an orphaned store on OUR data dir and `--verify` exited 1 | owner report 2026-10-12 (session triage) | **Done (2026-10-12, this commit)** — `references_dir` requires a whole path token: the occurrence must start at a token edge (line start, whitespace, quote, `=`) and end at a path edge (token end, quote, or `/` — the last so the node's own `dir/falkordb` store still matches). Fail-without-it: `store_ports_ignore_sibling_dirs_sharing_a_path_prefix` — the sibling's port appears in our port set on the pre-fix matcher, and the sibling's own dir still matches its store after the fix |
| D52 | **SSE response headers withheld behind the seed scan — intermittent degraded sync in long sessions** — GitHub issue #8 (owner, observed from a Codex MCP session): `backend_status` repeatedly flipped to `degraded: true, "SSE response headers timed out"` while `end_session` kept committing; reads fell back to the cached snapshot and recovered only when a later reconnect won the race. The reporter's own path trace (verified against 51aa32b): the `/v1/changes` handler awaited `graph_reseed_envelope` BEFORE constructing the SSE response, so a slow scan withheld the headers — and with them the 5s keepalives the client's R-C5 silence window rides on — past the client's 15s `stall_timeout`; `Sse::new`'s keepalives could not help before the response existed | GitHub issue #8, 2026-10-10 | **Done (2026-10-10, 8c68a3d) — the scan moved inside the response stream.** The handler's stream construction is extracted as `event_stream` with an injectable `SeedBuild` future; the anchor comment (headers + keepalives) flushes FIRST, the seed envelope builds and emits after it, and the reseed permit now spans the in-stream scan. A build failure closes the stream — the client's existing reseed-with-backoff path — instead of a pre-stream 503. The measurement the issue asked for ships with it: `elapsed_ms` logged on every seed build. Transport liveness still certifies nothing (the issue's acceptance guard): the client stamps freshness only when a frame applies, unchanged. Fail-without-it: `seed_stream_flushes_the_anchor_before_a_slow_seed_scan` holds the seed future behind a oneshot latch and drives the REAL `Sse` response body — the anchor frame must arrive within 2s WHILE the latch is held; the old handler shape cannot produce any frame until the scan completes (and the client's own degraded/reconnect/resync suites — sync, degraded_reads, backend_project_grant, 28 tests — pin the unchanged client semantics). **Boundary recorded:** the issue's fuller deterministic client-side regression (a controlled storage double delaying the authoritative-seed scan under a live client) needs a 61-method Storage seam the tree does not have; the latch-driven server test pins the same ordering property at the response boundary. First-boot hydration through an unusually slow scan can still surface the client's `InitialHydrationTimeout` (with its D47 diagnosis) — bounded, visible, and rare enough to leave |
| D51 | **Project-visibility rows written in standalone backend mode are never readable from a new session** — the owner's live bug report (2026-10-09, filed from a Claude Code session): a draft at the instruction block's DEFAULT visibility is accepted (LSN advances, the similar-to duplicate check even sees it) yet search returns empty and get_memory returns null; the same draft at org visibility is found — "every memory written by default can never be read." Root cause, TWO filters stacking: the standalone node's principal carried an empty project list (main.rs — the SSE feed and hydration dropped project rows server-side before any client cache could hold them; attached sessions reuse that principal, same gap), and a fresh client's read scope held only its own session's per-batch grants (D31's boot-seed reads the WAL, but backend-mode writes stamp nothing client-side; the post-ack grant covers only the writing session) | Owner's machine, 2026-10-09 | **Done (2026-10-09, f52152e) — three layers, one grant form, verified live on the reported repro.** (1) `memory_visible`/`relationship_visible`: a literal `"*"` entry in `project_ids` matches any project row — the wildcard sse.rs's discovery-region filter already honored, now honored by the authoritative §17.2 decisions. (2) `principal::standalone_visibility`: the standalone principal grants `["*"]` (max Org), so SSE/hydration deliver project rows to owner and attached sessions alike; `--principal-policy` (backend-node) is untouched — an admin MAY list `"*"` in a policy file, which now wildcards (documented semantics of an admin-authored file). (3) The wrapper exports `EXOCORTEX_STANDALONE=1` (owner and attached clients alike); the client joins `"*"` into its read scope ONLY for standalone-marked sessions — a plain `--backend` client against a policy-driven backend keeps D31's per-batch session grants, pinned by the existing `a_rejected_batch_does_not_grant_its_project` negative. Fail-without-it: `wildcard_project_grant_sees_every_project_row`, `exact_grants_stay_exact_beside_the_wildcard`, `wildcard_does_not_cross_tenants`, `wildcard_covers_project_visibility_relationships` (storage); `standalone_visibility_grants_every_project` (server); `standalone_marker_grants_earlier_sessions_project_rows` (client e2e: writer session, then a FRESH process with the marker finds the row). Verified live on the owner's personal graph — the reported repro, fixed: project write in one process, found by a fresh process on the first search. Riding in the same commit: the standalone restart budget 3 → 512 — five SIGILLs in nine minutes exhausted the old budget live (the report's second symptom: node and store stopped, degraded SSE, connection refused; the log line the report doubted was a stale-pid artifact, no new crash class), and every consumer now survives a supervised restart (D46 reconnects, D46c ingest retry, cache reseed), so the budget must outlast a working day of the runtime's residual fork deaths instead of converting them into session death. **Boundaries recorded:** the darwin runtime's fork SIGILLs remain (D50's boundary stands — upstream rebuild); install gotcha found while verifying — `cp`-ing a new binary over a running mapped one taints the inode (`cs_invalid_page` → SIGKILL on next exec); install via fresh-inode `mv` |
| D50 | **The remaining module forks SIGILL the supervised store's parent under node traffic — the 2026-10-07 session-death cascade:** D45 removed the BGSAVE fork, yet the owner's standalone stack still crashed every session: four store SIGILLs per boot (crash reports: faulting thread parked in `__psynch_cvwait` inside the module's `thread_do` — the fork corrupts a parked worker's condvar and macOS pthread traps it), each restart re-armed the next death, and end-of-session batches failed with an opaque `internal storage error` while the client served frozen reads. Two fork triggers remained: (a) the async-delete worker's `RedisModule_Fork` child, and (b) an undisarmable index-GC timer fork at ~39-56s post-boot (no `--loadmodule` argument — `FORK_GC_*` strings are dead embedded-RediSearch code; no `GRAPH.CONFIG` entry; no Cypher index `OPTIONS`; npm ships only `@falkordblite/darwin-arm64` 8.2.3-falkordb.4.16.2 and .3) | Owner's machine, 2026-10-07 | **Done (2026-10-07) — root-caused from the owner's crash reports, fixed in three layers, verified live on the real personal graph.** (1) Supervisor sends `GRAPH.CONFIG SET ASYNC_DELETE no` over RESP after every store boot (spawn and restart paths) — the ONLY accepted form: `0`/`false`/`off` are rejected, and it is not a load-time argument; `ASYNC_DELETE` is 12 bytes (a miscounted `$11` bulk length is a protocol error the server answers by closing the link — caught live by the first e2e). Fatal on darwin with the cause named, warn elsewhere. Fail-without-it: `async_delete_no_resp_spells_the_only_accepted_form` (exact bytes + per-token framing). (2) `exocortex-storage::fork_window` (D46b): the supervisor arms a 25-75s post-boot quiesce window; the falkor adapter's four command paths (`run_template`, `run_atomic_parts`, `upsert_batch_inner`, `delete_memory_fenced`) and the Dreams fire-drain poller `await_if_holding` through it, so the index-GC fork meets an idle store (6/6 isolated survivals on a real-data copy; idle instances fork at +43-51s and live). Fail-without-it: the three `fork_window::tests` gate-math tests. (3) Ingest `storage_retry` (D46c): `Backend` errors whose text contains "connect" (case-insensitive — covers "Could not connect", "Connection refused", and the falkor client's "This requested returned a connection error, however, we may be able to create a new connection…") retry 6× at a 2s gap; idempotent by construction (the relationship-target load is a read; the commit dedupes by batch key, a landed retry yielding `Duplicate` which the caller already handles). Verified end-to-end on the owner's personal graph: end_session that failed every time now commits (accepted + assigned_lsn advance, `backend_status` `degraded=false` across restarts). **Boundaries recorded:** the runtime can still SIGILL one-to-two times per boot window (instant-boot deaths plus forks under ungated ambient traffic — the permanent fix is an upstream falkordblite darwin-arm64 rebuild; the supervisor's bounded restarts plus the ingest retry ride through it); the Dreams fire drain mis-decodes BLMOVE nil-timeout as a `Response type not string compatible` WARN every ~5s (cosmetic, unfixed); the release-cut flake was `failing_spawn_kills_the_store_group` under back-to-back full-matrix load (D36 class, green standalone and in the passing reruns) |
| D49 | **--verify misses four checkable breakages** — GitHub issue #6: verify printed "all client-checkable preconditions hold" on a machine with (a) two orphaned redis-servers appending to one appendonlydir (could check live processes on the data dir / a supervised store whose supervisor is gone), (b) a stale `port` file from an earlier boot (could PING it), (c) NO harness wired (the row should appear and be RED, not be absent), and (d) an org/user partition mismatch — README examples say `my-org`/`me`, entrypoint defaults `personal`/`dev`, live data under `personal`/`gregory`: wiring the wrong pair silently starts an empty graph next door (verify could list partitions holding memories and warn when the configured pair has none but another does) | GitHub issue #6, 2026-09-29 | **Done (2026-10-01) — all four rows shipped, both recorded decisions taken.** New `verify_checks` module (pure, dep-free) + four rows in the client's verify: (a) `ps -axo pid=,command=` scan for store processes (redis-server/falkordb) naming the DATA DIR — one supervised store (live `.supervised.lock` pid) is ok; a store with a dead lock holder is RED (orphan); ≥2 are RED naming every distinct port (concurrent writers on one AOF); (b) the `port` artifact is PINGed (RESP; any reply = live, silent = RED stale) — the DECISION: keep and PING, never delete (nothing consumes it today; the R14-Cl3 note records the honest reason: a future owner may, and deleting an artifact a running store rewrites would flap); (c) the PRD-S6-promised harness row now EXISTS: RED when no known config (crushrc, ~/.claude.json, ~/.claude/settings.json) names a binary from this install's directory, green naming the harness + exe when one does; `EXOCORTEX_VERIFY_HARNESS_CONFIG=<file>` checks a custom harness's config; (d) `WalEntry` gains `org`/`user` stamped at append (serde-default empty on legacy entries), `Wal::partitions()` enumerates the ledger, and the row is RED naming every held pair when the configured `--org/--user` has no writes but others do. Fail-without-it: `client/tests/verify_rows.rs` (3 tests, driving the SHIPPED binary) — 0/3 with `main.rs` stashed (no rows existed), 3/3 on the fix; unit legs pin the ps parser, the harness matcher, and PING liveness against a real socket. The config skill's row-by-row verify interpretation documents all four. Gates: fmt, clippy -D warnings, full `cargo test -p exocortex-client --features exocortex-adapter-sdk/testing` green. **Boundaries recorded:** the store/port rows resolve the NODE's artifact directory (`store_artifact_dir` — the wrapper's `--data-dir D → D/falkordb` layout made the original client-dir reads false-RED healthy sessions, found and fixed in R14); the store row counts DISTINCT `--port` values (redis rewrites its process title; the watchdog shells duplicate its argv), and the port PING requires a RESP-shaped first byte (banner services used to count as stores); verify's exit code == red-row count is pinned on every verify_rows leg, the harness row needs dir+binary on ONE line, WAL decode is a single pass, and harness-config reads are bounded; the partition ledger covers offline/standalone writes (every standalone write rides the WAL) — pure `--backend` gRPC writes stamp nothing client-side; the harness row is fail-closed on unknown harnesses by design (S6's no-false-greens) with the env knob as the documented escape |
| D48 | **Installer: skill invisible to Claude Code; --dump-block not idempotent** — GitHub issue #7: the installer drops exocortex-config at `~/.agents/skills/` (Crush-only); Claude Code loads `~/.claude/skills/` and never sees it (README lists Claude Code FIRST). And `--dump-block >> CLAUDE.md` appends a second copy on every rerun with no version marker to find-and-replace. Fix shape: installer symlinks/installs into `~/.claude/skills/exocortex-config/` when `~/.claude/` exists (installer test leg for both shapes); the block grows `<!-- exocortex-block vX -->` markers and `--install-block <file>` replaces the marked region in place (idempotency test: run twice, one copy) | GitHub issue #7, 2026-09-29 | **Done (2026-10-01) — both halves landed; the word-gate trap resolved by trimming, not comment-exclusion.** (1) The block file stays marker-free (hash-stable, 292 words after a 5-word trim); `--dump-block` emits `marked_block()` — the block wrapped in `<!-- exocortex-block v1.0.0 -->`/`<!-- /exocortex-block -->` — and the NEW `--install-block <file>` flag rewrites the region in place via `playbook::install_block_into`: version-AGNOSTIC region match (an upgrade replaces an older version's region), the splice consumes the trailing newline (reruns do not accumulate blank lines), double regions heal to one, content outside the markers is untouched. The 300-word gate now binds the EMITTED form (`marked_block_within_word_bound`, 299/300) — markers included, so the trap (305 on 297) is paid by compression per the D41 precedent, recorded in PRD §11's length-target history; PRD §11.1, the D4 appendix quote, README, and the config skill all teach `--install-block` now. Unit regressions: `install_block_is_idempotent` (once == twice, exactly one region), `install_block_replaces_the_marked_region` (stale v0.0.1 region replaced, trailing content survives), `install_block_heals_double_regions`. (2) The installer seeds `~/.claude/skills/exocortex-config/SKILL.md` as a SYMLINK to the ~/.agents copy when `~/.claude/` exists (one source of truth; reinstalls refresh both), `EXOCORTEX_CLAUDE_HOME` is the sandbox knob, and the whole test exports it so no leg touches the runner's real ~/.claude. Installer-test legs: with-claude asserts `-L` + fixture content THROUGH the symlink; bare-home asserts nothing is created. Red/green proven: `sh scripts/test-release-installer.sh` exit 1 with `release-install.sh` stashed, exit 0 on the fix. Gates on the closing tree: fmt, clippy -D warnings, full `cargo test -p exocortex-client --features exocortex-adapter-sdk/testing`, `cargo xtask gen-playbook` (292-word block), installer test green. **Boundary recorded:** PLAYBOOK_VERSION stays 1.0.0 (the D41 precedent — content compression within a version line bumps nothing until the next release notes it); a pre-marker `>>`-appended block has no markers to find, so `--install-block` on such a file APPENDS one marked copy (the legacy duplicate must be deleted by hand once). **[R14]** `--install-block` now REFUSES unreadable target files (only NotFound starts empty — non-UTF-8 content used to be silently destroyed), and an unterminated begin-marker region is REPLACED to EOF instead of duplicating the block beside it (`install_block_replaces_an_unterminated_region`); the splice keeps a cursor (k regions, one pass) and the block body's marker self-reference is pinned |
| D47 | **Diagnostics: failures invisible end to end** — GitHub issue #5: (a) the node logs NOTHING by default (the SSE-hydration 503 branch was invisible until RUST_LOG=info — warnings/errors must reach stderr by default for a local service), (b) the client's "backend did not provide an initial graph seed within 15s" hides the server's actual 503 body (the error must carry the last HTTP status + body or connection error), (c) the MCP surface answers initialize/tools/list healthy while the store is down and every worker is failing (degraded store must surface to the agent — a health field on reads or tool-level errors; pairs with D46) | GitHub issue #5, 2026-09-29 | **Done (2026-10-01) — all three legs landed.** (a) ALL TEN workspace binaries (node, client, worker, 7 adapters — the reviewed pair generalized to the class) default their EnvFilter to WARN via `with_default_directive(LevelFilter::WARN).from_env_lossy()` — RUST_LOG still overrides, malformed values degrade instead of killing startup; fail-without-it the logging canary in `client/tests/logging_defaults.rs` (renamed by R14 to `binaries_installing_tracing_default_it_to_warn`; originally `binaries_log_warnings_by_default_rust_log_still_overrides` — a source canary over every workspace binary, stash-verified RED on the pre-fix tree naming the worker binary). (b) the SSE non-success arm now reads one bounded 1 KiB body chunk into the error ("SSE backend returned HTTP 503: storage unreachable…"), the subscriber records every stream failure (HTTP/transport/409/envelope-rejection) into a shared `StreamErrorCell`, and `SyncError::InitialHydrationTimeout` carries `{waited, LastSseFailure}` so the timeout renders the server's own diagnosis; fail-without-it `client/tests/sync.rs::hydration_timeout_carries_the_backend_http_status_and_body` (mock 503 + body; the pre-recording wiring was caught live by a probe — the cell stayed empty, the exact silent state — before the arm was wired). (c) reads stamp `sync: {degraded: true, last_error}` beside `snapshot_version` while the stream is failing (absent when healthy — healthy-path bytes stay registry-identical, pinned by the second test), transport activity clears the cell, and a new client-local `exocortex.backend_status` tool (the end_session precedent — excepted by name in `mcp_tool_list_matches_registry`, which gained the client-local-tool class) reports mode/snapshot_version/sync on demand; fail-without-it `client/tests/degraded_reads.rs` (4 tests: degraded stamping on all three reads, healthy-path identity, status tool shape incl. standalone, recovery clears — compile-red on the pre-fix lib: `with_sync_health`/`backend_status`/`StreamErrorCell` do not exist). README tool table documents the tool and the degraded stamp. **[R14]** the logging canary was renamed `binaries_installing_tracing_default_it_to_warn` (it claims what it checks; it now scans `src/bin/*.rs` too), the SSE error-body read is TIME-bounded (stall timeout — a wedged proxy parked the subscriber) and control-character-flattened (a hostile backend cannot forge log lines or inject into agent-facing error text), and the cell's production couplings are pinned through the real subscriber loop (`subscriber_records_and_clears_the_degradation_cell`). Gates on the closing tree: fmt, clippy (client, server, worker, all 7 adapters), full `cargo test -p exocortex-client --features exocortex-adapter-sdk/testing` green, `cargo xtask seam-inventory` green (41 seams). **Boundary recorded:** the node's own readiness probe flipping false is unchanged (D46 made it recover); D47 makes the CLIENT honest about a frozen snapshot — it does not add a readiness poll, the sync cell is the liveness signal |
| D46 | **Store consumers never reconnect after a supervised restart** — GitHub issue #4: after the supervisor restarts a crashed Falkor (same port, same token, verified healthy), the fire drain loops `broken pipe` once per second forever; the cache bridge, storage invalidation, post-ingest effects, and the discovery outbox all fail-retry against dead connections until process exit. A supervised restart is a designed-for event (max_restarts: 3) — every consumer must reconnect (rebuild connections on error rather than retrying over a pinned/pooled dead one), and the node must fail readiness loudly instead of serving healthy when it cannot | GitHub issue #4, 2026-09-29 | **Done (2026-09-30) — review-verified root cause** — `redis::MultiplexedConnection` never auto-reconnects (redis 0.27's own docs direct reconnect users to `ConnectionManager`; the `connection-manager` feature is not enabled). One pinned dead connection (falkor.rs `self.redis`, built once in `connect`) poisons: the fire drain (backend.rs builds two multiplexed conns once; the loop re-runs drain on the same dead conn — the once-per-second broken pipe), `drain_discovery_outbox` — which gates every `subscribe_invalidations`, wedging the cache bridge AND the cluster feed's resubscribe when any discovery row is pending — post-ingest effect delivery (the producer conn), and `storage.ping()` ITSELF, so `storage_ok` stays false forever and readiness 503s despite the store being back. The falkordb graph pool self-heals per connection; only the redis plane doesn't. Fix shape: enable redis's `connection-manager` feature and swap the three construction sites (falkor.rs, backend.rs fire drain pair) to `get_connection_manager()` — a feature flag on an existing dependency, not a new one — LANDED: redis's `connection-manager` feature enabled on the workspace dep (a feature flag on the existing dependency — PUBLISHING's rule-9 record amended in the workspace Cargo.toml comment) and the three construction sites swapped to `get_connection_manager()` (falkor.rs — including the `FalkorStorage.redis` field type — and the backend.rs fire-drain pair; `exocortex-dreams`'s `RedisFireQueue` connection type follows, and the backend_node test with it). Fail-without-it: `storage_pings_recover_after_a_store_restart` (supervisor tests, env-gated live) builds a FalkorStorage against a supervised store, hard-kills the store group, starts a NEW store on the SAME port and token (poll()'s restart by hand), and asserts the handle answers pings again within 15s — verified RED with the pinned MultiplexedConnection restored (panics "would ping dead forever" after the 15s budget) and green with the manager. The graph pool already self-healed per connection; every redis-plane consumer (fire drain, discovery outbox publish, health probe, post-ingest delivery) rides the manager now |
| D45 | **Supervised FalkorDB dies ~1s after start on an existing graph** — GitHub issue #3: on a copy of the real personal data dir the store dies ~1s after the client's seed subscribe (2/2 runs; 0/1 on an empty dir). Redis's own log shows a clean AOF load, one BGSAVE, then silence — no SIGSEGV/SIGBUS report (Redis prints those), so the process looks SIGKILLed or exited from inside the module. Data-dependent; the prior dual-writer state (#2) is a candidate cause but unproven (AOF loads without errors). The restarted server stays up — connects to D46 (consumers must survive the restart even when the first death is unsolved). Owner offered a debug build/core capture against the copy; repro harness should synthesize concurrent-writer AOFs locally | GitHub issue #3, 2026-09-29 | **Done (2026-10-05) — root-caused and fixed via the installation repair; the full trail: Review-verified mechanism hypothesis ranked — the review confirmed the seed-path calls are read-only GRAPH.RO_QUERYs that cannot kill the store from the Rust side, and that post-startup deaths get NO diagnosis (diagnose_exit runs only from wait_ping; poll() logs a bare WARN). Best-fit mechanism: the `--save 1 1` BGSAVE fires ~1s after the first dirtying change — on an existing graph the AOF replay dirties keys so the fork fires at exactly the observed moment, and an empty dir has nothing dirty (the 2/2-vs-0/1 split); the killer fits SIGKILL (OOM) or a FalkorDB-module exit during/around the fork over #2-corrupted state (Redis prints reports for SIGSEGV/SIGBUS/etc but not SIGKILL). Repro harness design: (a) the same dir with `--save ""` overridden — surviving implicates the fork; (b) a dual-writer harness (two 5beaf74-era boots on one dir, both append, kill, copy, boot once) to manufacture the corrupted precondition; (c) an empty-dir control (0/N must hold); (d) negative controls: a port-collision race (predicts a STARTUP diagnosis, not silence) and a client kill (predicts SHUTDOWN SAVE lines). LANDED: poll() deaths now carry the full death description (exit status + the store's own log tail via describe_death) at ERROR level — visible under the default filter, where the bare WARN was suppressed — and the budget-exhausted error includes the last death; `post_startup_death_carries_the_log_tail` pins it. The repro harness (`store_death_repro_harness`, env-gated on the runtime like the auth suite): the control leg asserts an empty-dir store survives its first dirty BGSAVE; the dual-writer leg demonstrably manufactures the 5beaf74 hazard (writer B serves concurrently on one dir via spawn_child, flock bypassed deliberately) then observes a fresh boot through its first dirty-save window WITH and WITHOUT snapshots (the discriminator, `save_policy` knob; the dirty phase writes plain SETs — CR-10 forbids Cypher outside exocortex-storage, and the real-data mode is what carries actual graph keys); `EXOCORTEX_REPRO_DATA_DIR` observes a COPY of a real dir; `EXOCORTEX_REPRO_SCALE` multiplies volume. RUN RESULTS (2026-09-30, local Redis 8.2.3 + bundled module): synthetic dual-writer corpora at default AND scale 3000 (~3M nodes, two interleaved writers) did NOT reproduce the death — both observation legs survived; a slow-boot artifact at scale (AOF replay > 10s) was found and fixed with a 60s observation startup budget. ROOT-CAUSED 2026-10-05 while repairing the owner's local installation (see the Done tail): the death is the `--save 1 1` BGSAVE fork — one dirtying write on the loaded real graph, fork at t+1s, the child SAVES SUCCESSFULLY and the PARENT dies with SIGILL (`Illegal instruction: 4`); identical data with `--save ""` survives the full read/write workload indefinitely (all 1,644 nodes live). The D47 WARN-default logging made the death loop visible live; the empty-dir controls never crashed because nothing dirtied. FIXED: the supervised store's default disables the RDB snapshot (durability rides the AOF, everysec — the snapshot was redundant); the harness knob still overrides. Fail-without-it: `supervised_default_disables_the_bgsave_snapshot`. Boundary: the precise faulting instruction is unconfirmed (needs a core capture — the standing offer; a FalkorDB/GraphBLAS multithreaded-fork hazard is the shape; the repro harness remains for an upstream report) |
| D44 | **Standalone is single-instance-unsafe: duplicate stores on one data dir; supervised Redis orphaned on exit; WAL single-writer** — GitHub issue #2 (the root of the cascade): nothing stops two harness sessions from each booting a supervised Falkor against the SAME data dir (documented wiring spawns one per session — concurrent sessions are the normal case); the supervised redis-server outlives its node (reparented to ppid 1 — live evidence: two orphans 28s apart on one AOF: corruption and lost-write hazard, last BGSAVE wins); and the client WAL (sled) locks its dir exclusively, so concurrent clients on the default --data-dir cannot coexist. Fix shape, staged: (S1) the supervised store dies with its node — a watchdog wrapper so an orphaned child kills itself when its parent is gone (portable pdeathsig emulation), plus an exclusive data-dir lock so a second standalone boot either ATTACHES to the live node via the published endpoint+secrets (S2, the right UX: reuse the port file and runtime env, client-mode against the existing backend, per-session WAL slot) or REFUSES naming the owner. Fail-without-it: two concurrent boots on one dir never produce two live stores; kill -9 the node leaves no orphan within the watchdog interval | GitHub issue #2, 2026-09-29 | **S1 Done (2026-09-30), S2 Done (2026-10-01) — both halves landed** — S1 (the safety half): `supervisor.rs` — (a) the store runs behind a watchdog shell in its own process group; a watcher subshell polls the supervisor pid and kills the store when it is gone, so a node dying without running Drop (kill -9, crash) can never leave an orphaned redis-server (the wrapper re-exports the store's exit status, so the D29 exit diagnosis keeps working, and the macOS DYLD bundling rides under a neutral env name the shell re-exports — Apple-protected interpreters scrub DYLD_* from their own environment); (b) teardown signals the whole group (`kill(-pgid)`) on Drop and on failed startup; (c) `spawn_supervised` takes an exclusive `flock` on `<data-dir>/.supervised.lock` — a second live store on one directory is refused, naming the directory and the remedy, and flock's auto-release means a crashed node never leaves a stale lock. Fail-without-it: `data_dir_lock_is_exclusive_and_released`, `store_child_dies_with_its_supervisor` (drives `spawn_child` directly so the error-path group kill cannot satisfy it; verified red with the watcher disabled: "the store outlived its supervisor"), `failing_spawn_kills_the_store_group`; the D29 diagnosis test stays green through the wrapper. One dependency, rule-9 recorded in PUBLISHING (`libc`: flock + group kill). S2 LANDED (2026-10-01, 13d1ec6): the refused second boot now ATTACHES. `acquire_data_dir_lock` returns the typed `DataDirOwned` error; the node's attach path reads the owner's `attach.json` (0600, atomic, published beside the graph with backend URL + bearer + HMAC + SSE key, loopback- and hex-validated fail-closed), probes the owner's node over gRPC Fingerprint with the owner's bearer AND an exact ontology-fingerprint match (retrying ≤20s while the owner is still binding — the lost boot race), publishes the wrapper's runtime env with the OWNER's credentials + `EXOCORTEX_ATTACHED=1`, and exits 0 without ever spawning a store. The wrapper exports the owner's creds, strips any user `--data-dir` from the forwarded args (clap rejects duplicates — found live), and appends a per-session WAL slot under its temp runtime dir, so concurrent clients never contend the default WAL's sled lock. Fail-without-it: `standalone_wrapper.rs::two_concurrent_sessions_share_one_store_via_attach` — env-gated live over the bundled runtime: two concurrent wrappers on one data dir, ONE store (counted by DISTINCT --port values: redis rewrites its process title and the D44 watchdog shells duplicate its argv — the naive pid scan double-counts, found live and fixed in BOTH this test and D49's verify row), session 2 serves MCP, a write through session 1 is searchable through session 2 — verified RED on the stashed tree (the exact 'another supervised store already owns' refusal) and GREEN on the fix; hermetic companions: `attached_session_reuses_owner_credentials_and_gets_its_own_wal_slot` (fake-bin wrapper leg) + supervisor units (`attach_record_round_trips_and_fails_closed_on_malformation`, `data_dir_conflict_is_the_typed_attach_signal`). The live test's cleanup is panic-proof (catch_unwind + marker sweep — an earlier run leaked a node+store for 11h when an assert panicked before the explicit kills). **S1 review edges:** the second-boot race is gone by construction (an attacher never spawns); the watchdog pid-reuse/restart-SIGTERM/port-gap/foreign-writer edges remain recorded S1 residuals, unclaimed here. **[R14]** the attach record is now PARSED before trust (plain http, no `@` in the authority, literal loopback host, explicit port — a userinfo URL passed the old prefix check and dialed off-loopback) with the one-impl `signing::decode_hex32` validating every secret; the gRPC probe is time-bounded (one hoisted runtime, 3s per attempt, `EXOCORTEX_ATTACH_DEADLINE_MS` tunable — a hung owner used to park past the promised 20s); the OWNER removes attach.json on exit (Drop guard); the wrapper accepts uppercase hex, cleans the per-session slot with `rm -rf` (kept loudly while `[pending]` writes remain — `rmdir` never removed a sled tree and pending entries were stranded), and never sweeps TMPDIR (measured 33s/run — recorded boundary); an UNGATED node-side test (`attach_tests::attach_publishes_owner_env_and_refuses_mismatched_owners`) covers the attach path without the runtime, and verify-release runs the live attach suite whenever the bundled runtime resolves **Review (2026-09-30) recorded S1 edges for S2 to close:** the watchdog polls the supervisor pid (pid reuse by the OS would orphan the store); a ≤1s window exists between node death (flock releases instantly) and the watchdog's reap where a racing boot could still double-write; the restart path's dying shell SIGTERMs a watcher that may be blocked in sleep ≤1s (pid reuse could mis-target); a restart can hit a port grabbed in the gap; and the flock cannot see foreign writers (a manually-run redis-server on the dir). S2's attach design removes the second-boot race entirely |
| D42 | **Ship a config skill with the install** — the D41 dogfood migration needed a pile of integration knowledge that lives nowhere a session can find it: which binary to wire (the entrypoint, never the raw client), where the instruction block rides, what each `--verify` RED row means, how to triage a dead MCP connection (probe the configured command with a JSON-RPC initialize, read the harness MCP logs), WAL-drain semantics (pending entries, terminal `InvalidTypeTriple` rejections at drain), and install-path differences (installer vs cargo vs checkout). Fix shape: the repo carries the skill at `.agents/skills/exocortex-config/SKILL.md` (auto-discovered by Crush inside the checkout), the release archive packs it, and the installer deploys it to `~/.agents/skills/exocortex-config/` (validated fail-closed like the model sidecar; not transactional — an inert markdown file on rollback is harmless, unlike a half-swapped binary). README's integration section points at it | owner request, 2026-09-28 (after the D41 session) | **Done** — `.agents/skills/exocortex-config/SKILL.md` (the wiring table for Claude Code / Crush / generic MCP, the instruction-block deployment, a `--verify` row-by-row interpretation including the pending-WAL signature and drain-time terminal rejections, dead-connection triage with the JSON-RPC probe, and install-path differences); `release.yml` packs `skills/exocortex-config` into every target archive; `release-install.sh` validates it fail-closed ("archive has no config skill") and installs to `${EXOCORTEX_SKILL_DEST:-$HOME/.agents/skills/exocortex-config}` after the transactional commit (outside the rollback set — an inert markdown file on a rolled-back install is harmless, unlike a half-swapped binary; stated in the script). Fail-without-it: `scripts/test-release-installer.sh` grows the skill fixture + installed-file assertions on both completing runs, and a new no-skill-archive leg that must be REFUSED with nothing installed (green: "fixed HTTPS origin, failure-atomic install, tamper refusal, and skill fail-closed"). README integration section points at the skill; deployed globally on the dogfood machine |
| D41 | **The instruction block must surface a dead memory connection, not skip silently** — found migrating the DF1 dogfood off the deprecated raw-client wiring: a full Crush session ran all day with the exocortex MCP server failed at startup (initialize EOF, logged 13:33) and every memory instruction silently skipped — nothing in the block tells an agent that absent `exocortex.*` tools are a fault to report. Fix shape: one clause in the block opening ("if they are absent, say so immediately — never skip memory silently"), paid for by compression inside the ≤300-word gate (297/300), mirrored in the full playbook intro and PRD §11 (which records the addition and the compression precedent). No installer or README wiring change was needed — the README already documents the entrypoint path the dogfood had bypassed; a Crush example was added next to the Claude Code one | DF1 dogfood migration, 2026-09-28 | **Done** — `playbook/block_v1_0_0.md` (+11-word clause, −11 of trims: "locally first/exactly", "search results"→"reads", "derives them from evidence"→"derives them", "Do not search on every turn"→"Don't search every turn", "surface any unfixable rejection"→"surface unfixable rejections", "printed by --verify"→"--verify prints", "At the end of every turn"→"At every turn's end"); `playbook/v1_0_0.md` intro; `agent-instructions-prd.md` §11 mirror + the length-target history note. Fail-without-it is the gate itself: the block is 297 words WITH the clause — the pre-change block (297 without it) is the repo's own history; `cargo xtask gen-playbook` green, consumer_contracts + stdio_smoke green. The dogfood crushrc migrated to the entrypoint (`exocortex --mode mcp-standalone`), draining the 79 pending WAL entries into the supervised backend — old memories verified searchable post-drain; 2 legacy D29-era entries terminally rejected as `InvalidTypeTriple` (offline-accepted, server-rejected — the documented drain-time check, audit-marked) |
| D40 | **Dreams abstraction rows are unpinned on the read surface** — round-13 finding R13-4: abstraction rows (carrier `General`, title "Abstraction of N <Type>", content = the first eight member titles) reach agents as ordinary rows, but no test pins retrieval through `search_memories`/`find_related` — only internal id/timestamp pins exist (`dreams/tests/abstractions.rs:198,220-243`). Fix shape: one test that runs a Dreams cycle over a fixture corpus and reads the abstraction back through the served snapshot (the dreams `testing` feature is the vehicle) | `docs/reviews/round-13-review.prd` (R13-4), 2026-09-27 | **Done** — `dreams/tests/abstraction_read_surface.rs`: runs a real consolidation cycle over the abstractions.rs fixture class, serves the committed graph through the ops registry handlers over a published LocalCache snapshot (the same entries MCP and HTTP dispatch through), and reads the abstraction back the way a session would — `search_memories` finds it by its own title carrying content + the `General` carrier label (S8), and `find_related` anchored on an Org member reaches it through the computed `Summarizes` membership as a full labeled row. Fail-without-it: without the cycle there is no such row and the search leg fails on zero hits (the test demands the row); without D35 the content/label legs fail (the stash evidence). Two dev-deps land with a rule-9 record inline (`dreams/Cargo.toml`: exocortex-ops + exocortex-cache, dev-only, no cycle — ops does not depend on dreams). Gates: fmt/clippy clean, `cargo test -p exocortex-dreams --test abstraction_read_surface` green |
| D39 | **Pin the ONLINE end_session ack through the real tool** — round-13 findings R13-1/R13-3: the online ack's most agent-actionable fields (rejections[] `code`+`detail`+`correction`, `local_validation_failed`, `unverified[]`, `similar_to[]` — and the `distinct` suggestion value, pinned nowhere today) are pinned at the wire/registry/HTTP layers but never through `exocortex.end_session` itself; the playbook's core rejection loop ("read the code and detail, fix, resubmit same turn") is unpinned on the surface agents actually call (the only stdio-level assertion checks a rendered substring). Fix shape: extend the ai1 consumer-contract harness to the `--backend` path — the `backend_project_grant` in-process backend node is the vehicle — pinning the ack fields through the real tool over an invalid batch, a near-duplicate batch, and the unverified list | `docs/reviews/round-13-review.prd` (R13-1, R13-3), 2026-09-27 | **Done** — `server/tests/online_ack_contract.rs` (`#[cfg(feature = "testing")]`): drives the SHIPPED client binary over gRPC against an in-process backend node and pins every online-ack field an agent acts on — the clean-batch shape (accepted/rejected/assigned_lsn, skip-serialize emptiness for `unverified`/`similar_to`), all three implemented suggestions (`duplicate` naming the existing row by citable id + title; `contradicts` for cross-type near-duplicates; `replaces` for same-type one-word updates), the §3.2 r3 honesty pair in one deterministic ack (`unverified[]` naming the unknown-target check BESIDE the server's structured rejection with `local_validation_failed:false`), and the local rejection loop (`InvalidTypeTriple` code+detail+correction, `local_validation_failed:true`, nothing committed). Enabling seam: `exocortex-server` gains a `testing` feature (the dreams precedent); `run_backend_node_inner` attaches the deterministic FakeEmbedder under `all(not(fastembed), testing)` — release paths unchanged (fastembed takes the real model; plain builds keep NO embedder, fail-closed). Fail-without-it: with the seam disabled the test fails red at the first hint assertion (verified 2026-09-29); the matrix invocation gains the flag (`verify-release.sh`, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md — whose trap note now names both mandatory features and the empty-binary skip shape). The `distinct` suggestion value is NOT pinned and never will be as-spec'd: the classifier emits only contradicts/duplicate/replaces — recorded as the round-13 spec-vs-code gap; and `unverified`-with-accept is unreachable through one hydrated principal (every visible row is cached), so the leg pins the honest deterministic pair instead |
| D38 | **Round 13 review: memory lifecycle consumer-adequacy + testing approach** — a scoped review round (`docs/reviews/round-13-review.prd`) over the two questions D35 exposed: (a) the memory lifecycle end-to-end (write → read → maintain → export/restore): for every stage, who consumes the output, and is the consumer's need pinned by an executable test or matrix criterion — not just cross-surface consistency; (b) the testing approach itself: where parity/byte-identity tests share one projection and structurally cannot catch a shared omission, where additive-safe subset assertions dominate, and which agent-facing payloads still lack a consumer contract. Findings flow back as rows under this round's IDs, the established lifecycle | owner request 2026-09-27 (D35 postmortem) | **Done** — `docs/reviews/round-13-review.prd`: 8 findings across both questions. Fixed in the round's closing commit: R13-5 (`backup_restore.rs` `hits_of` carries content; the wipe+restore round-trip asserts both bodies byte-equal — fails on the pre-fix (id,title) projection) and R13-7 (`gates.rs` oc/px/ac counts are EXACT, were ranges tolerating dropped rows; the xtask canary grows a dropped-oc variant that PASSES the pre-fix range check and FAILS the exact one — the variant is the fail-without-it). Opened D39 (online ack through the tool) and D40 (abstraction read-surface pin). Decisions recorded in the review: tags/summary/timestamps not exposed on reads in v1 (nothing promises them; revisit on demonstrated consumer demand); parity legs keep their transport-wrapping role with adequacy carried by consumer contracts (ai1/D39); result payloads are pinned by behavior contracts, not goldens. Companion work audited as already landed: D35, D36, D37 |
