| D31 | **Standalone search is blind to every memory written at the instruction block's default visibility** — reproduced live in the DF1 dogfood (crushrc client 0.4.1, `--org personal --user gregory`, no backend): `end_session` with a `visibility: "project"` draft acks (local_lsn advances) yet `search_memories` returns zero hits for the exact title, forever; the same batch at `visibility: "org"` is searchable. Root cause is a write-path/read-path asymmetry: `main.rs::org_visibility` builds the session `VisibilityContext` with `project_ids` empty, `end_session` pushes the batch's `project_id` into a LOCAL vc clone for that call only (`tools/end_session.rs:172-179`), while `search_memories` serves from the session vc — and `storage::memory_visible` requires `vc.project_ids.contains(project)` for Project rows (`exocortex-storage/src/types.rs:417-421`). Net effect: the shipped block's "Visibility: Default `project`" guidance makes every dogfooded memory unfindable (the standalone readback tests pass only because they write `org`). Fix direction: seed the session vc's project_ids in standalone personal mode (one-project org: the project is the org), or change the block's default for standalone, or both; a `fail-without-it` test is a project-visibility write followed by a search hit. Secondary observation from the same session, since resolved as NOT a defect: the one empty search right after the ack was whole-query substring semantics (search matches the contiguous query against lowercased title+tags), not a publish race — the F2 publish is synchronous inside `end_session` | Crush dogfood session, 2026-09-13 | **Done** — `client/src/mcp.rs`: the server keeps a session project set; offline `end_session` grants the batch's project into it BEFORE the F2 publish (cross-batch edge targets in the same project resolve too), and every read path (`registry_ctx`, preflight, the publish-time target lookup) uses the joined context. `client/src/main.rs`: standalone boot seeding collects the WAL's distinct non-empty project ids onto the session vc, so the scope survives restart. Empty `project_id` now normalizes to `None` at write (matching `team_id`'s existing filter — an unscoped row no longer claims a phantom `""` project). Fail-without-it: `standalone_readback.rs::project_visibility_write_is_searchable_in_session_and_across_restart` (project-visibility write → search hit in-session AND after restart; fails on the pre-fix tree with the fix stashed, passes fixed). Gates on the closing tree: `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo clippy -p exocortex-client --all-targets -- -D warnings`, full `cargo test -p exocortex-client --features exocortex-adapter-sdk/testing` (89 tests green). Backend mode untouched (project membership server-side is PLT1's shared-graph concern) |
| D30 | **The R-Lat3 allocation gate counts process-wide and can flake red** — the first CI run on the v0.4.1 closure commit (b804099, run 34565870901, 2026-09-11) failed `exocortex-cache --test alloc` with 4 allocations inside the R-Lat3 window where 0 are required; the same test passed twice in the d0a5e2b run (34441244949), the two trees differ only in scripts/docs (zero Rust delta), 0/60 runs flake locally on macOS, and the failed job's rerun went green in 24m9s — a one-off out-of-window allocation (background runtime / runner image) leaked into the `stats_alloc` global counter, which counts every allocation in the process, not just the test thread's. Hardening candidate: quiesce or pre-warm every in-process source around the window, or scope the count to the calling thread so the gate measures the read path alone | CI run 34565870901 (b804099), 2026-09-11 | **Done** — `cache/tests/alloc.rs`: the process-wide `stats_alloc` global allocator is replaced by a thread-scoped counting allocator (a `thread_local!` `Cell<u64>` incremented in `GlobalAlloc::alloc`; `dealloc`/`realloc`/`alloc_zeroed` ride the default impls, so same-thread counting is unchanged — only FOREIGN-thread allocations stopped counting), and the `stats_alloc` dev-dep is removed (this suite was its only user; MILESTONE_REPORT W11's mention stays as the historical record it is). Regression: `foreign_thread_allocations_do_not_count_in_the_window` places 64 foreign allocations DETERMINISTICALLY between the two reads (handshake via plain atomics — a channel send from the measuring thread would itself allocate inside the window) and asserts a calling-thread delta of 0. Fail-without-it: the identical scenario against the pre-fix process-wide harness, temporarily restored as a scratch suite and deleted before commit, failed red with 65 counted vs 0 allowed — deterministic, not a scheduling race. Gates on the closing tree: `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo clippy -p exocortex-cache --all-targets -- -D warnings`, full `cargo test -p exocortex-cache` (28 tests green) |
