oes not always trigger deferred tool discovery — pin the tool id or use interactive sessions; interactive sessions prompt normally. Concurrent-boot contention VERIFIED EMPIRICALLY: droid connected while three live personal stacks held the WAL (the wrapper attaches to the live supervised node instead of fighting for the lock; no orphans left after the probe). Remaining PLT1 work: the persistent-backend-node cutover decision (one node, every harness a `--backend` client) and R12-44's SERVER half below. Prior note (2026-09-19, R12-44) stands as the repo-work piece: the CLIENT half of project visibility over `--backend` is fixed; the remaining pilot-owned piece is the SERVER half — principal project membership, so SSE delivery (per-subscriber visibility filtering) actually carries project-visibility rows to their writers' peers. The old prerequisite decision (cut first vs pilot on main; installed client 0.2.2 staleness) is resolved by history: 0.5.1 shipped AND installed |
| D29 | **Packaged-standalone validation: supervised redis exits during startup (second invocation, release runner)** — found by the re-tagged v0.3.0 release run AFTER D27's fix cleared the first invocation. Sequence (run 33543324895, macos-14 arm64 Package job): `embedder-ok … max_tokens=512` ✓ → `Error: supervised redis-server exited during startup` → `standalone node exited before publishing its backend endpoint` → `test-standalone-live.sh: line 68: printf: write error: Broken pipe`. The SAME script + SAME pinned runtime (darwin-arm64@8.2.3-falkordb.4.16.3, SHA-512-verified in-run) passes 8/8 locally, so the failure is specific to the release runner or to back-to-back invocation state (leading suspicion: invocation 1's supervised redis/node not fully torn down — port or process cleanup race — when invocation 2 starts; the packaged validation runs the wrapper twice against one extracted layout). Fix requirement: the supervised-store supervisor must surface WHY redis exited (capture its stderr into the error) and the packaged validation must be robust to (or provably serialize) cross-invocation teardown. **Blocks the v0.3.0 GitHub release** (crates are on crates.io; tag v0.3.0 re-pointed at `07d4694`; no release/draft exists yet) | v0.3.0 re-tag release validation, run 33543324895 | **Done — five stacked defects, each invisible because the supervisor nulled the store's stdout (where redis logs). Primary, reproduced locally:** redis chdirs into `--dir` during startup BEFORE loading modules, so the wrapper's `dist/$DIST`-relative `--loadmodule` path resolves against the DATA DIRECTORY and dlopen fails ("no such file" -> "Can't load module ... server aborting", exit 1) on EVERY runner — the author's local 8/8 passed only because those invocations used absolute runtime paths. Fix: `resolve_paths` canonicalizes both runtime paths to absolute and fails fast naming the path when either is missing (`supervisor.rs::resolve_paths`; `resolve_paths_canonicalizes_relative_runtime_paths` + `resolve_paths_names_missing_runtime_files`; the CI-exact RELATIVE-layout invocation of `scripts/test-standalone-live.sh` failed pre-fix with the dlopen message captured and passes post-fix — the strongest fail-without-fix). **Secondary floors (proven binary facts; both runners would still fail after the path fix):** the darwin-arm64 `falkordb.so` declares `minos 15.0` (`otool LC_BUILD_VERSION`) so macos-14 can never load it -> arm64 release leg moved to `macos-15`; the linux-x64 `redis-server` references `GLIBC_2.38` symbols while ubuntu-22.04 ships 2.35 -> the linux leg keeps its ubuntu-22.04 BUILDER (shipped binaries keep their glibc 2.35 floor) and validates the packaged artifact inside the digest-pinned `falkordb/falkordb` trixie userland (glibc 2.41; libgomp/libssl3/libcrypto3/libstdc++6 verified present by executing the pinned image). **Diagnosability (the row's original fix requirement):** `wait_ping`'s exit path now names exit status (code or signal) plus the bounded 2 KiB tail of the store's own log with BOTH stdout and stderr joined to the sink — redis logs its startup output to STDOUT, which is exactly why the first stderr-only capture came back empty on run 33590545074 (`startup_failure_names_the_cause`, proved fails-by-stash). The cross-invocation-teardown suspicion is disproven: the ubuntu leg of run 33543324895 died on the FIRST invocation with no prior wrapper run, and the restart-durability leg (which exercises teardown) passes locally end to end. Floors recorded at every user surface (RUNTIME-MANIFEST.txt, installer refusal, README-install.txt, README, PUBLISHING.md; milestone deviation 21; the missing numbered deviation-20 entry the plan already cited was also written). Run 33590545074 (the re-tag with only the floors+path story) then exposed TWO further stacked defects, both fixed in the same closure: (a) the darwin-arm64 module dlopens Homebrew libomp and openssl@3 at absolute /opt/homebrew paths — absent on stock machines (runners, clean Macs); the runtime fetcher now bundles both as pinned ghcr.io bottle blobs (the last arm64_sequoia builds, minos 15.0, matching the module floor) beside the module, and the supervisor points the store child DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH at that directory (mechanism proven live with DYLD_PRINT_LIBRARIES against the real store binary; `store_child_receives_the_bundled_dyld_library_path` pins the constructed command — Apple-protected interpreters scrub DYLD_* so a stub cannot observe it); (b) the wrapper handed the background MCP client `<&0` and a non-interactive shell may give a background job /dev/null stdin (POSIX async-list behavior — reproduced with stubs inside the pinned container, both dash and bash): the client read instant EOF ("expect initialize or server/discover request"); the wrapper now duplicates its stdin to fd 3 in the foreground and passes `<&3` (stub-proven under dash, `scripts/exocortex`). Local end-to-end: full live script green on the CI-exact relative layout with the bundled runtime. **Shipped:** release run 33601757000 green end to end (correctness, all three build legs, release job) and `Exocortex v0.3.0` published 2026-09-02 with all six binary assets, checksums, and install.sh; the release note records that the live FALKOR_URL/REDIS_URL suites and the Docker chaos harness were NOT executed |
| D27 | **Standalone submit hang** — the intermittent `end_session` gRPC hang against a standalone node found by the v0.3.0 release validation (`docs/bug-prd-standalone-submit-hang.md`) | `docs/bug-prd-standalone-submit-hang.md` | **Done** — both halves closed. Deterministic half: dropped mortgage registration → fingerprint split → fail-closed hydration, fixed with the binary-level canary `the_binary_registers_every_shipped_pack`. Intermittent half: rmcp drops in-flight (and read-but-unpolled) tool calls at stdin EOF — fixed with the EOF-draining stdio reader `client/src/eof_drain.rs` (quiet = no in-flight call AND no dispatch in the 300ms grace window; 55s budget > the 20s+30s gRPC deadlines; `eof_drain.rs` unit suite pins withhold/grace/budget/passthrough), the deterministic no-runtime regression `client/tests/backend_eof_drain.rs` (in-process node behind a 400ms delaying proxy; proved failing-by-stash), and the live script sequencing write→commit→read with both ack and marker asserted (`scripts/test-standalone-live.sh`, 8/8 clean runs). The wire path was healthy throughout (h2 trace ~40ms); the HTTP/2-race and lost-wakeup hypotheses are disproven and recorded in the PRD. Re-tagged release run 33543324895 passed the first invocation (this defect's site); the hold moved to D29 |
