| PX2-S1 | **Spike — is `actions!` expressible in `macro_rules!`?** **Not blocked by Wave 0**; pure feasibility, needs no fingerprint work, can start immediately. `macros.rs:93` records that `macro_rules!` cannot tokenize past `;` inside a body — which is why `CREPE_RULES_SRC` is captured as `stringify!`'d text — and the `actions!` sketch in the palantir PRD §3.2 is semicolon-separated statements inside a body: the same shape that already did not work. Test `actions!` and `functions!` separately; `functions!` bodies are `datalog!`/`scheme!`, already text-shaped, and may be fine while `actions!` is not. **Three outcomes, each changing PX2's size:** (a) it expands — 1a proceeds as written; (b) bodies must be captured as text like Crepe rules — but Crepe rules work because the reasoning crate compiles them at build time, and there is no equivalent home for an Action body that must construct pack-typed memories and edges, so text-capture probably does not survive contact; (c) a proc-macro is required — a new crate, `syn`/`quote` as dependencies (AGENTS.md rule 9 record), and a materially larger PX2. **The semantic stake, not just the implementation cost:** if bodies are not type-checked at pack-compile time, P3's "Actions are typed transforms" weakens and D2's "the pack author cannot bypass the visibility ceiling" stops being a compile-time guarantee. Deliverable is a written decision in the PRD and this row, plus a throwaway `pack!` fixture demonstrating whichever outcome held | palantir-expansion PRD §3.2, §4.1 | **Done — outcome (a)**: both sections are expressible in plain `macro_rules!` (no proc-macro, no new deps). `actions!` bodies capture as tts and type-check in the pack crate (typed transforms and the ceiling guarantee stay compile-time); `functions!` text bodies capture exactly like `crepe_rules!`. Proof: `crates/exocortex-kernel/tests/actions_macro_spike.rs`; decision recorded in the PRD above the assumption it resolves; the `macros.rs:93` note is sharpened. Mechanical cautions for PX2: tt accumulators stay `tt`, entry arms after recursion arms, no identifier splicing (registry arrays) |
