**Open-row gates (stated 2026-09-02, revised 2026-09-03 after Wave 4
closed; keep current as rows open, park, or close):**

- **Locally satisfiable — an unattended run may take them in wave
  order:** Wave 5's D3 (federation — §24 q15 is the sharp form; the
  v1-locked constraints it cites are all still in place) and D15
  (row-level ACLs beyond the five labels). Wave 4's review landed, so
  the wave is open; neither row is small, and each one's first
  deliverable is the design decision its PRD § names — the
  common-subset fingerprint for D3, the authorization-DSL shape for
  D15 — before any code.
- **Credentials-gated — park and surface:** D19's remaining Linear
  live leg parks on the owner minting a LINEAR_API_KEY (the MCP
  authorization cannot ride the adapter); the GitHub leg ran on the
  owner's gh CLI token. Jira/Salesforce/ServiceNow extensions re-park
  on their own credentials when this row's Linear + GitHub legs close.
- **Demand-gated — park, never invent demand:** D14 (a deployment
  that demands 10M), D16 (a third pack proposed from outside), D26
  (scale pressure or the training direction being accepted), and the
  training-corpus direction itself (explicitly unresolved — an owner
  decision, not a row to drift into).
- Wave 5's D3/D15 inherit their wave; nothing there is special-gated
  beyond the wave rule above.
